\fs26 \cf3 My Response:\cb1 \
\cb4 The lesson of the fox and hedgehog story is that having a million smart ideas doesn't matter if you can't solve problems.\'a0\'a0The fox is a know-it-all, but the hedgehog keeps things simple.\'a0\'a0Also you shouldn't walk into holes while bragging about your brain power.\'a0\'a0The story applies today because a lot of people online act like experts about everything, but panic over stupid stuff.\'a0\'a0Sometimes quiet people show off less and help more.\'a0\'a0It applies to me too, because I sometimes overthink things instead of just doing the obvious solution.\'a0\'a0Like spending a million hours online looking for the perfect solution.\'a0\'a0And researching a homework assignment instead of starting it.\'a0\'a0The story shows that being calm is probably more useful than acting like a genius.\cb1 \
\
\cb4 Story:\'a0 \'a0\'a0
\fs24 A fox meeting a hedgehog asked him,\'a0\'a0\'93How many wits have you?\'94 And the hedgehog replied,\'a0\'a0\'a0\'93Three. But how many have you?\'94 Said the fox:\'a0\'a0\'a0\'93I have seventy-seven.\'94\'a0\'a0As they were talking and walking along, not noticing where they were going, they fell into a deep hole which the peasants had dug. The fox asked the hedgehog to save him. The hedgehog said,\'93I have only three wits. Perhaps you will save me first, then I will see about you afterwards,\'94 and he asked the fox to pitch him out of the hole. The fox did so, and then asked the hedgehog whether he could help him. The hedgehog said, \'93I cannot help you with three, if you cannot help yourself with seventy-seven.\'94 Gaster, M. (1915) Rumanian Bird and Beast Stories.\'a0\'a0
\fs26 Questions:\'a0 \'a0\'a0What do you think is the moral or lesson of the story?\'a0\'a0How do you think it applies to the world you live in?\'a0\'a0How do you think it applies to you personally?}